    "order": 1,
    "title": "Deduction/inference",
    "summary": "a conclusion you make by combining clues in the text with sensible reasoning",
    "explanation": "An inference is a conclusion that the writer does not state directly. You make it by combining evidence from the text with sensible background knowledge. A good inference is not a random guess: you should be able to point to a clue and explain how that clue supports your conclusion.",
    "examples": [
      "Text clue: “Keisha closed the window and pulled her sweater tighter.” Inference: the room or weather is probably cold. Evidence: she closes the window and adds warmth.",
      "A character repeatedly checking the door may suggest she is waiting anxiously for someone; the repeated action is the evidence.",
      "Use a short passage about school, community life, a Jamaican news topic or a Caribbean story, then point to the exact words that support the answer. Focus on the exact concept “Deduction/inference” and explain which part of the example shows it."
    ],
