    "title": "How relief affects settlement",
    "summary": "the shape and height of the land — for example flat, rolling, hilly or mountainous",
    "explanation": "Relief means the shape and height of the land. Settlement means a place where people live. Relief can affect settlement because steep or rugged land is usually harder and more expensive to build on and travel across, while flatter land often makes roads, buildings and services easier to develop. Relief is not the only factor — water, jobs, history, transport links and planning also matter — but it is one important physical factor.",
    "examples": [
      "Compare two hypothetical sites: Site A is flat beside a main road; Site B is on a steep isolated slope. Site A is generally easier and cheaper for roads, buildings and services, so it may support denser development.",
      "Flatter land often makes roads and dense settlement easier than steep mountainous land.",
      "Imagine comparing relatively flatter land around Spanish Town with the steep roads and slopes of the Blue Mountains. Building, travelling and moving goods are usually easier on gentler land than on steep terrain. Focus on the exact concept “How relief affects settlement” and explain which part of the example shows it."
    ],
